# BB — Agent Runbook
File: bb/docs/runbook.oot

When to reach for each bb command while building, debugging, or gating.
All commands fail closed (nonzero exit plus `ERR` line) on bad inputs.
Compiler resolution is OODA_COMPILER else OODAC_BIN; reads and temp files
stay under OODA_FS_READDIR/OODA_FS_WRITEDIR.

## A build or test binary fails

Rerun it under the recorder to capture the failure; success writes nothing:

```sh
bb run --record .blackbox/flight.json -- <cmd> [args]
```

Read the capsule (`command`, `exit_status`, truncated `output`, 64KiB cap).
For a one-turn root-cause diagnosis from a capsule or crash report:

```sh
bb autopsy <autopsy.json>
```

## A diagnostic names a fixable defect

Apply the autopsy patch guard, re-run `oodac check`, roll back on failure:

```sh
bb heal <autopsy.json>
```

The report is `{"ok":...}` JSON. `--dry-run` previews (see `bb help`).
Never hand-apply a patch the healer refuses: a rollback means the guard
did not verify.

## Before committing a compiler change

Gate on token drift between the old and new dumps:

```sh
bb diff <before.oo> <after.oo>
```

Exit nonzero means drift. Identical files print zero-drift status.
Lowering work additionally uses `bb diff-em` (tokens vs `.em`
bytecode) to catch lowering regressions.

## From an MCP client instead of the shell

`bb mcp --stdio` serves four tools: `blackbox_autopsy` (diagnosis
from a capsule path), `blackbox_diff` / `blackbox_diff_em` (drift checks
on a file), `blackbox_trace` (format a flight log). Same fail-closed
semantics as the CLI.

## Inspect and replay

`bb inspect` runs live tokens/check/build on one file and
prints the stage text (compiler diagnostics included). `emit-c`
is named residual (`ok:false`, exit 2). `bb trace`
prints a written `.blackbox/flight.json`; `bb replay` and
`bb watch` cover recorded flights and live directories.
